WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.670
- [Instructor] We are told
that a race car driver

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.610
has 28 cars.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.850
Each car has four tires.

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.990
He has to replace all
the tires on the cars.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.670
He has 22 tires right now.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:17.630
Estimate the total number
of tires he needs to buy.

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:19.750
So pause this video and
see if you can do that.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.380
And the key word here is estimate.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.970
You don't have to figure out exactly.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:25.540
You just need to figure out roughly

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:28.410
the total number of tires he needs to buy.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:30.300
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.030
And as I mentioned, the
key word here is estimate.

00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:36.980
And estimating is something
we'll do throughout our lives.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.710
I probably do it once a day at least.

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.140
And when you're estimating,

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.670
you want to make all the
numbers roughly right,

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.220
but also think about,
hey, what do I need to,

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:47.420
how do I need to them about them

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.930
in order to make the
computation a little bit easier

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.690
or a little bit friendlier?

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.700
So if I wanted to figure out

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.680
exactly how many tires he needed to buy,

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.210
I would say, hey, he has 28 cars.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.740
He has 28 cars.

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.320
Each car has four tires.

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.730
So each car has four tires.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.530
So if you wanted to know the
total of number of tires,

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.260
you would multiply 28 times four.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:13.560
And then it says he has to replace

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.530
all the tires on the cars.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:16.660
And if he didn't have any,

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.890
this is the number that
he would have to replace,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.410
but it says he has 22 tires right now.

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.040
So if we were trying
to figure out exactly,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.050
you would say 28 times four,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.790
that's how many he needs to replace,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.740
but he already has 22,
so you subtract that out,

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:32.860
and this whole number would
be the number he needs to buy.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.250
Now this calculation isn't so easy to do,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.340
especially if you're doing it
on a napkin or in your head.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.730
But that's why we're going to estimate.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:41.910
So if we're estimating, we say, hey,

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:44.830
what's a friendlier number than 28?

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.430
Well, 28 is pretty close to 30.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.930
So I could say, so if I say,
hey, 28 is pretty close to 30.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.860
So that's our way of making
the 28 a little bit friendlier.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:57.380
Four is already pretty easy to deal with.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.300
And then the 22, well,
that could be a lot easier

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.100
to deal with, if instead of it being a 22,

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:06.840
if that were, let's say that's
approximately equal to 20.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.590
So 22 is approximately, roughly equal,

00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:12.800
that's what these squiggly
equal signs mean, to 20.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.010
And so that simplifies
the calculation a lot.

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:19.690
We could say that this thing
up here is roughly equal to,

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:21.657
he roughly has 30 cars,

00:02:21.657 --> 00:02:25.151
and each of those cars need four tires,

00:02:25.151 --> 00:02:28.550
and then he's going to figure
out the total number of tires

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.800
he needs to replace, but
then he subtracts out

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.150
the number that he already has.

00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:34.520
So instead of subtracting out 22,

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:37.580
we're gonna subtract out
roughly how many he already has,

00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:38.770
roughly 20.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.470
And this computation
right here is a lot easier

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:42.590
to do in our head.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:47.497
Three times four is 12,
so 30 times four is 120.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:51.090
So this part right over here is 120.

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.624
And then you subtract 20 from that.

00:02:53.624 --> 00:02:58.624
Well, 120 minus 20, well,
that is going to be 100.

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:03.610
So he roughly needs, if I were
to estimate the total number

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:07.623
of tires he needs to buy,
it would be about 100 tires.

